00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.829 align:start position:0%
 
battlefield has been one of my favorite  franchises ever since i would sneak  downstairs onto the family computer to  play 1942  almost 20 years ago almost 20 years ago  the franchise has obviously come a long  way since then  and from what we've seen of 2042 so far  is making yet  another leap from the franchise that a  lot of the fans may be  intimately familiar with so what does  that mean for the series  dice is calling this its next generation  of battlefield so it makes sense that it  will make a departure from the games  that came before it  and while it may be early days so far  from what i've seen it seems like the  objective is to make a battlefield  experience that's new  but has enough for veterans of the  series to stick around as well  a lot of the emphasis was put on  creating battlefield moments that the  franchise has become so famous for  those experiences are quintessentially  tied to the series  the larger than life takedowns  destruction and kills that can create  either  epic moments that stick with you long  after the round was won  or more bizarre almost slapstick  instances  that sit at complete odds to  battlefield's otherwise  largely serious and grounded gameplay  and i am all for that my immediate  reaction was pure oil  hell yeah more battlefield and while i  understand that this is a move that may  be influenced by  keeping the battlefield experience  interesting and shareable online  an aspect that a lot of shooters have  really thrived off of  in recent years just look at twitter and  all the war zone clips  it also means this next entry in the  series is as much  a playground as it is a virtual field of  combat  it also appears that alongside that  action sandbox there is also a huge  drive towards more diversity  variety and dynamism in the interactions  players have within battlefield  and in the ways that they are able to  play now let me see at this point i'm so  glad the franchise is returning to a  modern slash near future setting i  really enjoyed my time with battlefield  1 and 5 but i always gravitate back to  bf4  and i think the reason behind that is  that the modern or future backdrop  always provides more variety  than the titles from historical  conflicts is this a slight pull away  from battlefield being known as one of  the  realistic first-person shooter options  in the fps genre  i mean maybe some of the things that  dice are adding  can sort of be seen as a little bit  silly like the overly dramatic weather  the wing suits robot dogs  rising sea levels dwindling resources  no no some of those are real but if the  rest are implemented in that same old  battlefield fashion that  everything else is then i can't see why  these new maybe  over-the-top wrinkles and tools and  resources  and options and gadgets can't  comfortably fit  into the game by bumping to the near  future and creating its own setting and  backstory  dice no longer has to stick to conflicts  between countries  powers or already known factions it may  be without a single player campaign  something that i'm honestly not too  fussed about losing if the multiplayer  makes up for it  but the story they're telling via their  online experience has players taking the  role of a non-patriotic soldier  it's a natural avenue to create more  diversity in the individual combatant  as well as their equipment vehicles and  even settings  basically being a soldier of the world  means that you can come from  any background use any equipment and  have  any experiences to bring into the  battlefield and the maps and locations  are only possible with this direction  i mean hell yeah i want to wingsuit from  buildings and ride a tornado  damn right i want to chase enemies on  atvs through sandstorms and darn  two in your boy wants to zipline across  skyscrapers  none of that could really be done  without a move like this  and exploring the near-ish fictional  timeline without going as far as  revisiting 2142  is the perfect place for it and while  you can sort of see the real world  parallels that have  influenced this fictional world it can  still be matched with battlefield  quote-unquote  realism as well as having that  playground experience  this is after all the series where every  soldier has a parachute to deploy  at any time atvs are launched into  helicopters and pilots eject from  fighter jets  to snipe at enemy planes to me it's  always felt like these were moments that  you  could do in battlefield but now i get  the feeling that these are things dice  actively  wants you to do which again goes hand in  hand with this new playground that it's  building  during the press event where we got to  see a little of the game just before the  live stream announcement the folks at  dice were using the term battlefield  moments  a lot it's a term that they've used  across marketing for the franchise in  the past all the way since  around bad company 2 but it definitely  feels like an element they're  really driving forward with now and  veterans of the series might remember  battlefield bad company 2  was the most sort of slapstick silly  that the franchise has ever been  and that's what birthed battlefield  moments they want that same  creativity almost silliness again with  2042  for example being able to call in  vehicles anywhere on the map is going to  make  some stupid daft massive atv jumps while  tanks being dropped on top of buildings  just to shoot down choppers  and i admit while i wasn't too sold on  the premise at first i will hold my hand  up and say  that i do in fact want to throw c4 onto  my robot gun turret dog  and launch it off of a building in the  middle of some blade runner-esque city  now i have no way of knowing this for  certain but i get the impression that in  some of these design meetings the  decisions weren't so much  will this work in a battlefield game  more  will this be bloody cool in a  battlefield game  and i firmly believe that there is room  for the whole spectrum  especially with the matches being so  much bigger than before on massive maps  with 128 players  and why the hell not make room for the  daft memorable moments as well as the  intense fire fights and squad play  that sticks with you this can be your  battlefield  however you want to play whatever you  want to accomplish in the game being one  of 64 players on your team in a battle  of 128  gives you space to experiment and  experience new things  at your own pace new elements like  vehicles having new seats that actually  do stuff let new players always feel  useful  and more variety and bigger space in  more maps can let  soldier hone in on a preferred playstyle  additions to modes like conquests with  wider objectives and sectors over the  maps and the opportunity for more varied  gameplay on each map is so exciting  for the longest time a lot of  battlefield maps have been connected to  either vehicle or infantry combat  as part of their identity but i'm super  stoked to be able to experience  every facet of gameplay within the same  instance and dice also talked about what  it calls varied pacing  which aims to create both a hectic  feverish fights that the whole team  takes part in  as well as the smaller skirmishes over  objectives now this is also a grand  assumption i don't know this for sure  but i'm expecting we'll be seeing a  whole slew of customization options for  your soldiers and vehicles  after all battlefield 4 had skins and  battlefield 5 had their own uniforms and  more detailed cosmetics  since this is a live service game it  makes sense that the content will be  dropped in the same way or via their new  seasonal battle passes  and from what i could see in the trailer  there were all sorts of uniforms pieces  of equipment helmets and  camouflage adorning the combatants now  i'm not in love with everything i've  seen so far  part of being able to admire something  is seeing its flaws right that's  probably something my therapist said but  the on-the-fly weapon customization for  example who's  carrying all those scopes and barrels  and magazines around  personally i'd prefer the traditional  loadout system  respawn change your weapons but saying  that  battlefield 2042 so far is  ticking a lot of boxes for me there are  doses of nostalgia in there for veterans  of the series like  base jumping throwbacks to devaman peak  there's also a bunch for players that  are fairly new to the franchise and  seemingly a lot of cool new stuff  for those who have not yet experienced  glory in battlefield and i think this  push towards the playground sandbox and  dice's renewed mentions of those  battlefield moments  are going to be really important to keep  the player base healthy  and the game talked about social media  is always  filled with those clips and notable  moments from games like warzone and  fortnite and they do a lot  to keep the player base coming back and  introduce new players and remind people  that this game does in fact exist come  play it and battlefield really pioneered  that stuff with the battlefield moments  they wanted you to do  all the over-the-top silly stuff and  create stories around them  so it makes a lot of sense that they  want to be back in the conversation now  if that comes hand-in-hand with a bigger  broader and more assorted battlefield  experience then as a longtime fan  i'm all bloody for it the younger me  playing 1942  would really go bananas seeing this game  i hope you enjoyed the video if you have  your own thoughts and opinions please  let me know in the comment section down  below or reach out to me on twitter at a  regular dave i'd love  to hear what your thoughts are be you a  battlefield a veteran or someone just  joining the franchise either way make  sure you subscribe because we're gonna  have a lot more coverage  over the next few weeks and months but  until then thank you so much for  watching  and i'll see you next time  [Music]  [Music]  you

